---

<Layout title="Target ROAS by Margin: Set Profit Goals | Visible Ads" description="How to set your Google Ads target ROAS from contribution margin and the profit you want to keep, with a worked example and the mistakes to avoid." image="/images/og/target-roas-by-margin.png" schema={schema}>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="py-20 lg:py-28 bg-gradient-to-br from-navy to-navy-light relative overflow-hidden" data-animate>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 dot-pattern opacity-5"></div>
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative">
      <nav class="text-sm text-slate-light mb-8">
        <a href="/" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-white transition-colors">Blog</a>
        <span class="mx-2">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Target ROAS by Margin</span>
      </nav>
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="inline-block px-4 py-1.5 bg-electric/20 text-electric-light text-sm font-medium rounded-full mb-6">Strategy</span>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-heading font-extrabold text-white leading-tight mb-6">Target ROAS by margin: setting profit goals in Google Ads</h1>
        <p class="text-lg sm:text-xl text-slate-light leading-relaxed">Your target ROAS is the single number that decides whether Google Ads makes you money or quietly loses it. Most accounts pick that number by guessing. This guide shows you how to derive it from your margin and the profit you want to keep. The target then has a reason behind it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The guesswork problem -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-white" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">The guesswork problem</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">Most accounts set their target ROAS by guessing. The target is copied from last month's result, rounded up to a figure that feels ambitious, or borrowed from an article that quoted a number with no context. None of those methods has anything to do with whether the campaign is profitable. They just feel like a target.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">The trouble with a guessed target is that it hides both directions of error. Set it too low and the platform buys expensive conversions that lose money on every order. The report still shows sales coming in. Set it too high and the bidding system stops entering auctions it could have won profitably, and volume drains away for no reason. Either way you cannot tell. A guessed number has no break-even underneath it to measure against.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A target ROAS should be derived, not invented. It comes from two things you already know or can work out: your margin, and the share of revenue you want to keep as profit. Once those are on the table the target stops being a hopeful round number and becomes an instruction with a reason behind it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Break-even is the floor -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-sky-tint" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">Your break-even ROAS is the floor</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">Before you can set a target, you need the floor it has to clear. That floor is your break-even ROAS: 1 divided by your contribution margin. A 40 percent margin gives 2.5x, a 25 percent margin needs 4x, and a 20 percent margin needs 5x. Below it, every extra sale costs you more than it returns.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">Contribution margin is the selling price minus cost of goods, payment fees, shipping and returns. A softer figure makes the floor read lower than it is. The <a href="/blog/break-even-roas" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">break-even ROAS guide</a> shows how to build it.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A 4x target loses money on a 20 percent margin, because break-even there is 5x. <a href="/roas-vs-profit" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">ROAS vs profit</a> includes a calculator you can run against your own margins. Knowing your floor is what turns a target from a guess into a decision.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Deriving the target -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-white" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">Deriving your target from margin and profit</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">With the floor known, the target is the floor plus the profit you want to keep. Break-even tells you where you make nothing. Your target ROAS lifts you above it by enough that the campaign hands you a margin after the ad spend is paid. The size of that lift is a business decision, not a Google one.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">A product with a 40 percent contribution margin breaks even at 2.5x. At exactly 2.5x the campaign washes its face and leaves nothing. Suppose you want to keep roughly 15 percent of revenue as profit after the ads are paid. To do that on a 40 percent margin you need the campaign to return closer to a 4x ROAS, because the extra revenue per pound of spend is what funds the profit you are holding back. So the target for that product is not a guessed 4x, it is a 4x you can explain: 2.5x to break even, plus enough on top to bank 15 percent.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-8 leading-relaxed">The table below targets a different profit share on each row, shown in the last column. Notice that the same 4x target that is healthy on a 40 percent margin is a loss on a 20 percent margin, where break-even alone is 5x. This is the whole reason a single copied number is dangerous: the right target is a function of the margin. The margin changes from product to product.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-navy text-white">
              <th class="px-5 py-4 font-heading font-semibold text-sm">Contribution margin</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-4 font-heading font-semibold text-sm">Break-even ROAS</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-4 font-heading font-semibold text-sm">Example target ROAS</th>
              <th class="px-5 py-4 font-heading font-semibold text-sm">What it leaves</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-gray-700">
            <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
              <td class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-navy">20 percent</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">5.0x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">6.5x to 7x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">About 5 to 6 percent of revenue kept after ad spend</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
              <td class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-navy">25 percent</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">4.0x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">5.5x to 6x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">About 7 to 8 percent kept</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
              <td class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-navy">40 percent</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">2.5x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">About 4x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">Roughly 15 percent kept after ad spend</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50">
              <td class="px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-navy">50 percent</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">2.0x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">3x to 3.5x</td>
              <td class="px-5 py-4">About 17 to 21 percent kept</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Read the table across, not down. The high-margin row can profit at a lower target ROAS and therefore take more volume, while the thin-margin row has to hold a much higher target just to make anything. That is the insight a guessed number throws away. It is the reason the next step is to feed real margins into the account rather than a single average.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Setting tROAS in Google Ads -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-sky-tint" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">Setting the target in Google Ads and giving it room to learn</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">A derived target is only useful if the platform can act on it, and Google Ads bids to the conversion values you send. If those values are revenue, the target ROAS is measured against revenue and your margin never enters the maths. The cleaner approach is to pass contribution (profit after product cost and the other variable costs) as the conversion value, so a high-margin sale is worth more to the algorithm than a low-margin sale at the same price, and the target you set is a profit target rather than a revenue one.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">Whichever value you feed, give the target room to learn. Target ROAS is a Smart Bidding strategy, and it needs enough conversions and enough time to model the auctions before its output means anything. Change the target and the campaign re-evaluates every bid. A large or sudden move can send it back into learning and rattle performance for a week or more. Move in steps of around 10 to 15 percent, then wait for it to settle before judging the result.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two things undermine even a well-set target. The first is thin conversion volume, where the strategy has too little data to bid reliably and the numbers swing week to week. The second is dirty tracking: duplicated conversions, wrong values or a misjudged attribution window will quietly point the target at the wrong goal. Fix the data first, then trust the target.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Different targets by margin band -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-white" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">Different targets for different margins</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">One target ROAS across the whole account is a compromise that suits nothing. It sets a single goal for products whose break-even points are miles apart. The high-margin lines are held back below what they could profitably spend. The thin-margin lines are pushed past their floor and bleed money. The blended number can look fine on the account summary and still be wrong for almost every product inside it.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">The fix is to group products into margin bands and give each band its own target. High-margin lines get a lower target and are allowed to chase more volume, because they profit at a lower ROAS. Thin-margin lines get a stricter target that keeps them above break-even. You do not need a target per SKU, which would be unmanageable. A handful of bands, each with a target derived from the margin at its centre, captures most of the benefit.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">This gets harder as the catalogue grows. The margin spread across thousands of products is exactly where the profit is won or lost. We cover how to band and structure a large catalogue without drowning in campaigns in <a href="/high-sku-advertising" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">advertising a high-SKU catalogue</a>. The principle stays the same at any size: match the target to the margin, not to the account average.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- New-customer vs blended -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-sky-tint" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">New-customer targets versus blended targets</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">A blended target treats every order the same, whether it comes from someone buying for the first time or a loyal customer placing their fifth. That is fine if you only care about the single transaction, but it undersells acquisition. A first order is worth more than it looks when the customer comes back, and a blended target cannot see that.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">This is where lifetime value lets you bid harder. If your average customer buys three times over their life, a first order that only breaks even is still worth winning, because the profit arrives on the repeat orders. A new-customer target ROAS lets you set a lower, more aggressive goal on first-time buyers specifically, spending more to acquire them while holding your existing-customer traffic to a stricter target. Google Ads supports this directly through new-customer acquisition goals, so the split does not have to be manual.</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The one caution is to base the aggression on real repeat behaviour, not hope. If you set a low new-customer target on the assumption that buyers will return three times and they only return once, you are simply overpaying for acquisition. Pull your actual repeat rate before you lower the target, and revisit it as the data matures.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Common mistakes -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-white" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">A few errors show up again and again when accounts move to a margin-based target. Each one is easy to check before your next reporting cycle:</p>
      <ul class="space-y-4 mb-6 text-lg text-gray-700">
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric shrink-0">&bull;</span><span><strong class="text-navy">One blended target across all products.</strong> A single account-wide figure underspends your high-margin lines and overspends your thin ones. Band by margin and set a target for each band.</span></li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric shrink-0">&bull;</span><span><strong class="text-navy">Ignoring margin altogether.</strong> A target ROAS set without a break-even floor beneath it is a guess. Work out 1 divided by your contribution margin first, then build the target on top.</span></li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric shrink-0">&bull;</span><span><strong class="text-navy">Pushing the target too high, too fast.</strong> A big jump sends the campaign back into learning and volume can collapse. Move in steps of 10 to 15 percent and let each change settle.</span></li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric shrink-0">&bull;</span><span><strong class="text-navy">Using a soft margin figure.</strong> Leave out fees, shipping or returns and your break-even reads lower than it is, so a target that looks safe is actually below the floor.</span></li>
        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric shrink-0">&bull;</span><span><strong class="text-navy">Setting and forgetting.</strong> Margins move with cost prices, discounts and returns. A target that was right last quarter can be wrong this one, so revisit it as the numbers change.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Getting the target right depends on seeing profit clearly in the first place. That is the job of the mapping work we do before touching any bids. That margin-to-ad-data join is the backbone of our <a href="/how-we-audit" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">Profit Contribution Framework</a>, and it is what turns a target ROAS from a hopeful number into one you can defend.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- High target -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-gray-50" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-6">{highTarget.question}</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">{highTarget.lead}</p>
      <ol class="space-y-4 mb-6 text-lg text-gray-700">
        {highTarget.items.map((item, i) => (
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-electric font-bold shrink-0">{i + 1}.</span><span>{item}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">{highTarget.after}</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Seeing a high ROAS on the dashboard but little profit? That is a different problem, covered in <a href="/roas-vs-profit" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">high ROAS, low profit</a>. To find the floor itself, use the <a href="/blog/break-even-roas" class="text-electric font-semibold hover:text-electric-dark underline decoration-electric/30">break-even ROAS guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-sky-tint" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-8">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer p-6 font-heading font-semibold text-navy text-lg hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors">
              {faq.question}
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-electric shrink-0 ml-4 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">{faq.answer}</div>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Related Services -->
  <section class="py-16 lg:py-20 bg-white" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-heading font-bold text-navy text-center mb-8">Related Services</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
        <a href="/google-ads" class="group bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-md border border-gray-100 ring-1 ring-electric/10 hover:ring-electric/30 hover:shadow-lg transition-all duration-300">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-2 group-hover:text-electric transition-colors">Google Ads</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">Profit-first PPC management built around your real margins.</p>
          <span class="text-electric text-sm font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-1">Learn more <svg class="w-4 h-4 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
        <a href="/blog/profit-first-ppc" class="group bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-md border border-gray-100 ring-1 ring-electric/10 hover:ring-electric/30 hover:shadow-lg transition-all duration-300">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-2 group-hover:text-electric transition-colors">Profit-First PPC</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">The hub guide to managing Google Ads on profit, not revenue.</p>
          <span class="text-electric text-sm font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-1">Learn more <svg class="w-4 h-4 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
        <a href="/how-we-audit" class="group bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-md border border-gray-100 ring-1 ring-electric/10 hover:ring-electric/30 hover:shadow-lg transition-all duration-300">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-heading font-bold text-navy mb-2 group-hover:text-electric transition-colors">How We Audit</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm mb-3">The Profit Contribution Framework that maps profit before bids.</p>
          <span class="text-electric text-sm font-medium inline-flex items-center gap-1">Learn more <svg class="w-4 h-4 group-hover:translate-x-1 transition-transform" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Partner Badges -->
  <section class="py-12 bg-sky-tint border-y border-gray-100" data-animate>
    <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <PartnerBadges compact />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="relative py-20 lg:py-28 bg-navy" data-animate>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 dot-pattern opacity-5"></div>
